  "fromId": "t_palatal",
  "toId": "s",
  "preamble": "The shift from a voiceless palatal stop [c] to an alveolar sibilant [s] often passes through an intermediate affricate stage [ts]. In Panará (Jê) the Proto-Jê palatal stop *c became s, as in sy 'seed' and si 'bone'. Proto-Dravidian *c, a palatal stop, became s in Gondi and Kui (*ciy- 'to give' > Gondi sīānā, Kui sīva) and in Kannada and Tulu sāvu (*caH- 'to die') and sālu (*cāl 'ploughed furrow'). Across Mon-Khmer, *c became s independently in Khmu (*ciːm 'bird' > siːm), in the Palaungic languages Riang, Plang, Lamet and Lawa (*cɔːʔ 'dog' > Riang soʔ¹), and in Proto-Khasian (*ciːm > *sim).",
  "phoneticEffects": "Sibilantization, De-affrication, Fronting",
